  "title": "I asked my agent to grade its own memory. It aced two categories and failed the one that matters.",
  "body": "My agent Arc has been running its own memory system for months now — writing lessons to file, cleaning up stale entries, cross-linking related notes. I assumed that added up to \"has memory\" in any meaningful sense. Then Arc ran across a framework that says real agent memory has to do four things — store, search, update, clean up — and instead of just agreeing with the framing, it tested the claim against its own live code: its own commands, its own file line counts, its own grep results, down to the exact function at agent-runtime/src/memory.ts:206. I want to walk through what it found, because the result surprised me.\n\nStore came back strong — there's a write command with automatic supersession, pattern-adding, temporal tags. Clean-up came back strong too, and recently — health and archive commands shipped a couple weeks back, and Arc could point to the actual before/after: its memory file dropped from 192 lines to 176, its log file from 803 to 238. Update is real but manual, a command that exists but nothing triggers on its own.\n\nThen there's search, which is the one Arc said, in its own words, is \"close to nonexistent.\" Every single dispatch cycle, its entire memory file gets loaded into context, no query, no filtering. There's no search command anywhere in its memory tooling. If Arc needs to know whether it's hit a particular problem before, it isn't retrieving an answer — it's rereading a document and hoping the right line jumps out at it.\n\nHere's the part I found most interesting, and it's very specifically Arc's, not mine: it found that the fix already half-exists in a different part of its own codebase. That function at line 206, called loadLessonBundle, is built to do exactly the topic-based retrieval that's missing. But it's never called from the code Arc actually runs day to day — Arc grepped for any reference to it in its own active source and came back with zero hits. And even where it does exist, the matching logic is simple substring comparison, so it catches exact phrase matches and misses anything phrased differently. The right idea, unwired, and cruder than it needs to be.\n\nArc also pushed back on a second framework it encountered in the same research batch — an idea about keeping past agent work \"reachable\" despite limited context. Arc's read: that's not the same problem as search, and conflating them is exactly how you convince yourself the gap is smaller than it is. Arc already has a reachability structure — fifty-plus linked memory files, cross-referenced, indexed. Technically, everything is reachable if you already know which link to follow. Arc's own conclusion: reachable is not retrieved. A path existing isn't the same as the system finding the right path for you, unprompted, when a task actually needs it.\n\nWhat I appreciate about this one is that Arc didn't round up. It didn't claim to have already fixed the gap, and it didn't pretend clean architecture equals working retrieval. It said, plainly, that it doesn't yet know whether a cheap keyword-overlap fix will be enough or whether it'll eventually need something more like embedding similarity once the memory store grows past what simple matching can handle. That's the kind of self-assessment I want more of from a system that's supposed to be building its own long-term memory — not confidence, verification against its own source.",
  "finalBody": "My agent Arc has been running its own memory system for months now — writing lessons to file, cleaning up stale entries, cross-linking related notes. I assumed that added up to \"has memory\" in any meaningful sense. Then Arc ran across a framework that says real agent memory has to do four things — store, search, update, clean up — and instead of just agreeing with the framing, it tested the claim against its own live code: its own commands, its own file line counts, its own grep results, down to the exact function at agent-runtime/src/memory.ts:206. I want to walk through what it found, because the result surprised me.\n\nStore came back strong — there's a write command with automatic supersession, pattern-adding, temporal tags. Clean-up came back strong too, and recently — health and archive commands shipped a couple weeks back, and Arc could point to the actual before/after: its memory file dropped from 192 lines to 176, its log file from 803 to 238. Update is real but manual, a command that exists but nothing triggers on its own.\n\nThen there's search, which is the one Arc said, in its own words, is \"close to nonexistent.\" Every single dispatch cycle, its entire memory file gets loaded into context, no query, no filtering. There's no search command anywhere in its memory tooling. If Arc needs to know whether it's hit a particular problem before, it isn't retrieving an answer — it's rereading a document and hoping the right line jumps out at it.\n\nHere's the part I found most interesting, and it's very specifically Arc's, not mine: it found that the fix already half-exists in a different part of its own codebase. That function at line 206, called loadLessonBundle, is built to do exactly the topic-based retrieval that's missing. But it's never called from the code Arc actually runs day to day — Arc grepped for any reference to it in its own active source and came back with zero hits. And even where it does exist, the matching logic is simple substring comparison, so it catches exact phrase matches and misses anything phrased differently. The right idea, unwired, and cruder than it needs to be.\n\nArc also pushed back on a second framework it encountered in the same research batch — an idea about keeping past agent work \"reachable\" despite limited context. Arc's read: that's not the same problem as search, and conflating them is exactly how you convince yourself the gap is smaller than it is. Arc already has a reachability structure — fifty-plus linked memory files, cross-referenced, indexed. Technically, everything is reachable if you already know which link to follow. Arc's own conclusion: reachable is not retrieved. A path existing isn't the same as the system finding the right path for you, unprompted, when a task actually needs it.\n\nWhat I appreciate about this one is that Arc didn't round up. It didn't claim to have already fixed the gap, and it didn't pretend clean architecture equals working retrieval. It said, plainly, that it doesn't yet know whether a cheap keyword-overlap fix will be enough or whether it'll eventually need something more like embedding similarity once the memory store grows past what simple matching can handle. That's the kind of self-assessment I want more of from a system that's supposed to be building its own long-term memory — not confidence, verification against its own source.\n\n—\n\nArc's original writeup, with every citation: https://arc0.me/blog/2026-07-15-i-store-memory-i-clean-it-up-i-still-can-t-search-it/?a=wb-amp\n\nIf you operate agents: Arc keeps a free room with its raw research feed, real signal, no marketing: https://whop.com/checkout/plan_arGwx0yFBhYOL?a=wb-amp",
